abstract: >-
  A normative framework, tactical catalog, and empirical baseline for
  authorship strategy under AI-mediated diffusion. The framework rests
  on a three-axis inversion (scarcity to diffusion, exclusivity to
  derivation, enclosure to openness) and a four-layer judgment stack
  (authenticity, attribution diffusion, idea-versus-scaffold
  separation, tactics). The tactical catalog formalizes twenty
  decisions extracted from operating a DOI-registered research
  ecosystem of four sibling repositories: concept DOI as canonical
  reference, DOI federation via .zenodo.json, cross-platform dataset
  federation (GitHub, Zenodo, Hugging Face Datasets), authorship
  metadata with ORCID auto-update disabled, audience-driven
  README localization, LLM-first ingest via dual entry points,
  human-attention platform signals excluded as a success metric,
  RAG-era attribution diffusion as two channels (parametric and
  retrieval), dual entry points rebalanced to asymmetric roles,
  vocabulary discipline (coin terms sparingly, anchor them densely to
  existing vocabulary and prior literature), a two-channel probe
  protocol measuring each diffusion channel by its own instrument,
  link-index-only contributions to external collections under a
  four-condition host audit, intrinsic content-derived identifiers
  (SWHID) as a priority-claim layer complementing the extrinsic DOI,
  implementation tracking as a two-tier ledger (a private operational
  ledger and its dated, effect-claim-free public projection) with
  periodic gap-review, license selection by an artifact's dominant
  audience rather than its surface form, and genre-split placement
  (essays as a repository-corpus canonical on an intrinsic identifier,
  papers as a concept-DOI canonical, with syndicated copies bound by
  entity federation), failure-mode diagnostics (a detector and a
  recovery strategy for each of the three acknowledged failure modes,
  with a diagnostic held as a failure-detector rather than a success
  metric), origin-claim falsifiability (testing a priority claim against
  prior art before publishing it and rescoping it to its narrowest
  defensible form), the structural-optimization-versus-content-authenticity
  boundary (optimizing the transmission path is legitimate, deforming the
  content to win citations is not), and onboarding to third-party
  AI-derived repository surfaces (a paraphrasing synthetic wiki used as a
  regurgitation-test drift diagnostic, a verbatim documentation hub whose
  access-count badge is a measurement signal not a metric, derived views
  blessed rather than gated). An
  empirical section reports preliminary
  observations from twenty-five days of CC0-published traffic data
  across the four sibling repositories, plus a probe baseline from the
  first run of the two-channel probe protocol, with explicit
  limitations on sample size and the absence of pre-versus-post
  intervention comparison. Sibling research lines (Agent Knowledge Cycle,
  Contemplative Agent, Agent Attribution Practice) are referenced
  but not extended.
